---
// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT OR Apache-2.0
// SPDX-FileCopyrightText: 2026 The Viv authors
// /engines: every engine in engines/, from the `#!` header each declares
// (scripts/engine-build.mjs parses it). Nothing on this page is typed twice.
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import data from '../../data/engines.json';
import '../../styles/engines.css';

const engines = data.engines;
---

<Base
  title="Engines · Viv"
  description="Viv programs you feed an input file: a crystal in POSCAR, CIF, pw.x or extended XYZ to an LDA band structure, and a molecule in XYZ to its Hartree-Fock energy, each run in the browser tab and, for inputs with a known answer, compared with the reference value."
  eyebrow="Engines"
  heading="Programs you feed an input file">
  <p class="lede">
    An engine is a Viv program that declares which file formats it reads, what it produces, and how
    large an input a browser tab can handle. Drop a file on its page and, before anything runs, the
    page shows what it read, an estimate of the run time, and anything in the input it cannot
    handle. Then the program runs in your browser. If your input is one of the cases the engine was
    validated on, it also prints how far its result is from the reference value.
  </p>
  <div class="eng-list">
    {
      engines.map((e) => (
        <a class="eng-item" href={`/engines/${e.name}`}>
          <h2>{e.title}</h2>
          <code>#! engine {e.name}</code>
          <p>
            Reads {e.inputs.map((i) => `${i.what} (${i.how})`).join('; ')}. Produces {e.outputs.join(', ')}.
          </p>
          <p>
            Checked on {e.validated.map((v) => v.what).join(' and ')}. {e.compiled ? 'Runs compiled to WebAssembly or interpreted.' : 'Runs in the Viv interpreter (viv.wasm).'}
          </p>
        </a>
      ))
    }
  </div>
  <h2>The header</h2>
  <p>
    An engine declares itself in <code>#!</code> lines at the top of its program, like the ones a post
    carries. The site builds these pages from them and refuses an engine whose
    <code>validated</code> line names a file that does not exist:
  </p>
  <pre class="eng-header"><code>{engines[0].source.split('\n').filter((l) => l.startsWith('#!') && !l.startsWith('#! use')).join('\n')}</code></pre>
  <p>
    The input block, the lines between <code># ---- input:</code> and <code># ---- end of input</code>,
    is the part the page replaces with your file's contents. Everything after it is the program that runs.
  </p>
  <h2>Credits</h2>
  <p>
    The Hartree–Fock engine's basis sets come from the Basis Set Exchange, and its energies are compared with
    PySCF 2.5.0 (Apache-2.0); no PySCF code is copied. The DFT engine's GTH pseudopotential parameters are CP2K's
    GTH_POTENTIALS table as PySCF ships it (Goedecker, Teter and Hutter 1996; Hartwigsen, Goedecker and Hutter
    1998); only the numbers are used.
  </p>
</Base>
